WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.320 --> 00:00:04.230
So in this video I want to cover some of

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:06.789
the VHDL statements um and approaches

00:00:06.799 --> 00:00:08.950
that we will use in writing test benches

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:11.589
for simulation in VHDL.

00:00:11.599 --> 00:00:13.669
And I'm going to start by talking about

00:00:13.679 --> 00:00:15.829
the the definition of physical types in

00:00:15.839 --> 00:00:18.230
VHDL. So quite often we want to use real

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:20.470
units um when we're talking about our

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:23.670
design. Uh and so we can use the type

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:27.029
statement to define units. There are two

00:00:27.039 --> 00:00:29.750
inbuilt uh units predefined in the

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:32.310
standard library. These are for time.

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:34.470
Okay. So this is so so this is the

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:36.069
statement. It's a type statement and you

00:00:36.079 --> 00:00:37.670
define a range and then you define your

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:39.270
units. In this case it's based upon

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:41.830
femtoseconds and then we define picoseconds in

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:44.549
terms of femtoseconds and so on all the way

00:00:44.559 --> 00:00:46.389
up to an hour here. So we define the

00:00:46.399 --> 00:00:48.069
different units and we can then use

00:00:48.079 --> 00:00:51.350
those in our statements. Um the second

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:53.430
one that's predefined is power which is

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:55.430
obviously based upon microwatts or

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:57.189
nanowatts and on all the way up

00:00:57.199 --> 00:00:59.029
equivalently.

00:00:59.039 --> 00:01:01.910
So units cannot be used directly in

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:03.590
synthesizable design. They don't make

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:06.149
any make any sense really. Um if you

00:01:06.159 --> 00:01:07.429
want to use these when you're doing

00:01:07.439 --> 00:01:08.950
synthesizable hardware you would

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:11.510
basically normalize them or divide. So

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:14.230
if you had say a seconds you could do 10

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:16.550
seconds. You could divide it by I don't

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:18.789
know let's say microseconds or some other

00:01:18.799 --> 00:01:20.630
unit and it will give you an integer and

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:22.789
that integer could then be synthesized

00:01:22.799 --> 00:01:26.390
of course as as a binary number. Um but

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:28.630
you you can't use these directly in your

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:30.390
synthesizable line. You can't assign a

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:32.710
value of two nanoseconds to a register.

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:35.670
You have to scale it. Um depending in

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:37.030
that case it would depend on the clock

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:39.109
frequency. You can define your own units

00:01:39.119 --> 00:01:41.109
as well. And a common one that I I've

00:01:41.119 --> 00:01:42.789
used quite a lot is I've defined units

00:01:42.799 --> 00:01:48.149
for frequencies.

00:01:48.159 --> 00:01:50.870
So the state I want to talk about signal

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:54.069
assignment with specified delay um or or

00:01:54.079 --> 00:01:55.590
different ways of doing this. So these

00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:57.510
this is for really for simulations where

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:00.389
you want to and test benches where you

00:02:00.399 --> 00:02:02.389
want to have the simulation wait for a

00:02:02.399 --> 00:02:04.310
certain length of time and then do some

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:06.230
signal assignment set some value in a

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:08.229
signal.

00:02:08.239 --> 00:02:11.190
Um this is not synthesizable. So because

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:13.030
you cannot as I said you can't actually

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:15.270
have these delays they don't make sense

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:17.430
in a synthesizable design. So it

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:18.869
basically you do your signal assignment

00:02:18.879 --> 00:02:21.350
and you have after and a time period and

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:23.830
a unit of time. So for example a here A

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:26.949
assigned B after two nanose

00:02:26.959 --> 00:02:29.510
uh in a process. What that will do is

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:31.589
the process will wait for two nanoseconds

00:02:31.599 --> 00:02:33.910
and then do the signal assignment. So

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:35.990
you can use these to to construct a

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:38.630
waveform um in a process

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:40.470
by by changing signals after different

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:42.869
lengths of time.

00:02:42.879 --> 00:02:45.110
The second approach you can use is wait

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:47.830
for and that basically and then do some

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:49.589
statements. So wait for a time period.

00:02:49.599 --> 00:02:51.430
Again this is not synthesizable of

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:53.670
course. So you can have wait for two

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:56.070
nanoseconds and the process will then wait

00:02:56.080 --> 00:02:58.150
and then continue executing or going

00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:01.670
down the process uh after a two nanosecond

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:03.110
delay.

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:05.830
And so we use these two quite a lot uh

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:08.710
in writing uh test benches.

00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:10.550
I just also want to mention a couple of

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:11.589
other statements. These are

00:03:11.599 --> 00:03:13.110
synthesizable or can be used in

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:14.630
synthesizable designs. And I'll show

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:17.270
some examples uh that we haven't come in

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:18.869
the wait statement. So we have wait

00:03:18.879 --> 00:03:22.149
for waits for a certain length of time.

00:03:22.159 --> 00:03:25.190
We can have wait on a signal. Wait on x

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:27.750
will basically wait until x has changed.

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:29.190
Obviously also very useful for

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:31.350
simulation. this and I'll show you this

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:34.229
can be used in a in a process provided

00:03:34.239 --> 00:03:36.070
the process doesn't have a sensitivity

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:38.789
list. Uh and the same with wait until

00:03:38.799 --> 00:03:40.789
wait until will wait until some

00:03:40.799 --> 00:03:42.789
condition. So wait on waits for a signal

00:03:42.799 --> 00:03:45.589
to change. Wait until waits for a

00:03:45.599 --> 00:03:48.070
condition to met be met. So for example

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:50.149
wait until rising edge of clock would be

00:03:50.159 --> 00:03:52.390
an example. Just to be clear here

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:55.270
assigned delays I'm going to keep saying

00:03:55.280 --> 00:03:59.509
this do not synthesize.

00:03:59.519 --> 00:04:01.110
So when we talk about so we want to

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:03.910
write simulations to test our designs uh

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:06.070
before the circuit construction and in

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:07.750
the case of the exercise I've given you

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:09.270
you have to write one simple one but

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:11.110
I've given you a load of test benches to

00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:14.470
test your own designs out um so these

00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:16.390
basically the idea of a test bench is it

00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:18.229
provides a set of inputs to your unit to

00:04:18.239 --> 00:04:20.789
your entity that you've defined observes

00:04:20.799 --> 00:04:23.670
the output values and tests them um

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:25.510
right so it'll me take the output values

00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:27.430
and check if they are what's expected

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:29.510
that's basically what we do with test

00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:33.030
bench. So a test bench is a model uh is

00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:35.510
a structural model with two components.

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:38.870
Uh we basically have the test uh itself

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:41.430
the tester which will generate uh the

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:43.430
vectors

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:46.230
um and read the outputs and generate

00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:48.070
reports as well. So quite often we'll

00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:49.830
want a report of how it's performed and

00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:51.350
then the unit under test is the unit

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:53.510
you're testing. So I call that uut unit

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:55.430
under test the model under test. So this

00:04:55.440 --> 00:04:56.950
is the basic structure. We have the

00:04:56.960 --> 00:04:59.430
tester generating signals connected to

00:04:59.440 --> 00:05:01.749
the unit under test and then the outputs

00:05:01.759 --> 00:05:03.510
from that come back to the tester which

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:05.590
can then check the values against some

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:08.310
expected values.

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:10.629
Uh the tested uh vectors are applied to

00:05:10.639 --> 00:05:12.710
the unit under test in reports and and

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:15.270
they can be in the case of the exercises

00:05:15.280 --> 00:05:16.790
in this introductory course they're

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:18.790
actually just written in VHDL using some

00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:21.029
of the statements I've just mentioned.

00:05:21.039 --> 00:05:22.950
More generally these could be generated

00:05:22.960 --> 00:05:25.830
from a file. Um so you might have a file

00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:27.830
of test vectors and the tester will read

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:31.029
data from a file and use that to test to

00:05:31.039 --> 00:05:34.150
send signals to the unit under test. Um

00:05:34.160 --> 00:05:36.550
or they or or they could be generated

00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:38.310
using pseudo random techniques. There's

00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:40.230
lots of approaches can be used depending

00:05:40.240 --> 00:05:44.230
on what you're trying to test for.

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:46.310
Uh and of course the top level describes

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:47.909
the connection between the tester and

00:05:47.919 --> 00:05:50.230
the under test. Note at the top level a

00:05:50.240 --> 00:05:53.510
test bench has no inputs or outputs and

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:55.990
all it's right. So it's it's a top level

00:05:56.000 --> 00:05:57.749
unit with no inputs or outputs. It just

00:05:57.759 --> 00:06:01.590
runs the test and checks values coming

00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:04.790
back. So here is uh I've got time define

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:07.749
a test bench for an AND gate.

00:06:07.759 --> 00:06:11.189
So um in this case uh the top level is

00:06:11.199 --> 00:06:13.110
the tester. That's typically what I use

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:15.029
in these simple cases. I don't I don't

00:06:15.039 --> 00:06:17.350
need a separate entity just to connect

00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:19.830
the two. So here I have the component

00:06:19.840 --> 00:06:21.670
under test is an AND gate. I want to test

00:06:21.680 --> 00:06:23.350
the AND gate circuit. So I've got the

00:06:23.360 --> 00:06:24.710
component definition. It's got two

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:27.749
inputs A and B and a result. Um I've got

00:06:27.759 --> 00:06:30.309
signals to represent A and B and the

00:06:30.319 --> 00:06:31.990
result as well. So these

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:33.110
are basically representing the

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:34.950
connections.

00:06:34.960 --> 00:06:37.670
Uh and then I have to instantiate the

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:40.870
unit under test. So UUT um unit under

00:06:40.880 --> 00:06:42.950
test entity. I give it a name. Um,

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:44.710
sorry. I I declare what it is. It's

00:06:44.720 --> 00:06:47.909
work.and_gate. Um, that's not really

00:06:47.919 --> 00:06:49.110
needed because I've got a component

00:06:49.120 --> 00:06:50.469
definition, but that's basically saying

00:06:50.479 --> 00:06:54.070
it's in the the same um package. Um, I I

00:06:54.080 --> 00:06:55.749
connect the signals together. So, I'm

00:06:55.759 --> 00:06:58.230
connecting the A port of the AND gate to

00:06:58.240 --> 00:07:00.230
our A signal, B port to the B signal,

00:07:00.240 --> 00:07:02.950
and so on. Then I have a stimulus

00:07:02.960 --> 00:07:05.350
process. Uh, so this is

00:07:05.360 --> 00:07:08.390
very common. And it basically says begin

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:11.749
assigns values to A and B and waits for

00:07:11.759 --> 00:07:14.309
10 nanoseconds and then assigns some new

00:07:14.319 --> 00:07:18.230
values to A and B and so on. So this uh

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:19.990
test bench is simply setting the

00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:21.909
signals. It doesn't do anything else. It

00:07:21.919 --> 00:07:23.990
sets signals. If you run this in the

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:25.670
simulation, you could look at the

00:07:25.680 --> 00:07:27.350
waveform, right? So you can use the

00:07:27.360 --> 00:07:30.629
waveform viewer to look at the signals

00:07:30.639 --> 00:07:32.790
with the in the exercises I've given

00:07:32.800 --> 00:07:35.110
you. uh what you'll find is I've

00:07:35.120 --> 00:07:37.749
actually uh declared that all of the

00:07:37.759 --> 00:07:39.670
signals will be stored in the

00:07:39.680 --> 00:07:42.150
simulation. So you can view all of the

00:07:42.160 --> 00:07:44.070
signals down the hierarchy you want if

00:07:44.080 --> 00:07:45.110
you want to if you want to have a look

00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:47.110
at it. So if you were to run this and

00:07:47.120 --> 00:07:49.909
then um using the simulation task in in

00:07:49.919 --> 00:07:51.589
the exercises

00:07:51.599 --> 00:07:53.350
uh what you would get is a waveform that

00:07:53.360 --> 00:07:55.189
you could then view and obviously check

00:07:55.199 --> 00:07:58.710
manually by by by looking at it.

00:07:58.720 --> 00:08:00.469
However, more more commonly, we actually

00:08:00.479 --> 00:08:02.070
want the test bench to read the values

00:08:02.080 --> 00:08:04.469
coming in and check them. So for that,

00:08:04.479 --> 00:08:06.230
we would use the assert statement. So

00:08:06.240 --> 00:08:08.070
this is another statement. Again, this

00:08:08.080 --> 00:08:09.430
is not something you would uh

00:08:09.440 --> 00:08:13.510
synthesize. So you do an assert um and

00:08:13.520 --> 00:08:15.350
define a value that you're asserting.

00:08:15.360 --> 00:08:16.950
You're basically that's you want that to

00:08:16.960 --> 00:08:20.070
be correct. And then a report. So if the

00:08:20.080 --> 00:08:23.029
if the condition is not met, it sends

00:08:23.039 --> 00:08:25.189
out a report, right? That prints that

00:08:25.199 --> 00:08:27.430
out on the terminal if you want. And

00:08:27.440 --> 00:08:29.510
then you can define a severity and there

00:08:29.520 --> 00:08:30.869
are different levels of severity

00:08:30.879 --> 00:08:32.709
declared.

00:08:32.719 --> 00:08:36.310
Um so at the severity level and so when

00:08:36.320 --> 00:08:37.670
you run the simulation in some

00:08:37.680 --> 00:08:40.630
simulators you can declare uh so the

00:08:40.640 --> 00:08:42.230
severity level is printed on the report

00:08:42.240 --> 00:08:44.070
as well. In some simulators you can

00:08:44.080 --> 00:08:46.949
declare which severity levels terminate

00:08:46.959 --> 00:08:49.269
you know stop the simulation and some

00:08:49.279 --> 00:08:52.070
just just print a report.

00:08:52.080 --> 00:08:54.790
Um in um I think in XSIM you don't have

00:08:54.800 --> 00:08:58.550
that option. I think error and worse um

00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:02.336
stop it from what I remember. Um so this

00:09:02.346 --> 00:09:03.590
is how we would do that.

00:09:03.600 --> 00:09:06.550
Here we have a I've I've obviously left

00:09:06.560 --> 00:09:08.630
out some of the VHDL to make it fit on

00:09:08.640 --> 00:09:10.470
the screen.

00:09:10.480 --> 00:09:13.030
Um but this is basically what what I've

00:09:13.040 --> 00:09:14.949
done here is is instead of setting the

00:09:14.959 --> 00:09:17.190
two input signals A and B as two

00:09:17.200 --> 00:09:18.630
separate assignments, I've actually

00:09:18.640 --> 00:09:22.230
declared a a vector of two bits.

00:09:22.240 --> 00:09:26.230
uh and the uh these two bits um can be

00:09:26.240 --> 00:09:28.630
then be set to set the input. So rather

00:09:28.640 --> 00:09:31.269
than set uh separate signals I'm using a

00:09:31.279 --> 00:09:33.190
vector that connects to the input ports.

00:09:33.200 --> 00:09:34.949
It just means I could when we look down

00:09:34.959 --> 00:09:37.990
here you can see I've got input IP is

00:09:38.000 --> 00:09:40.230
assigned the value 0 0 rather than do a

00:09:40.240 --> 00:09:41.990
assign b and b assign zero. So that's

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:44.790
all I've done here. Uh we wait for the

00:09:44.800 --> 00:09:46.389
time for certain we have to wait we wait

00:09:46.399 --> 00:09:48.230
for some length of time. I've picked 10

00:09:48.240 --> 00:09:51.030
nanoseconds um just because that happens

00:09:51.040 --> 00:09:52.870
to correspond to a 100 megahertz clock

00:09:52.880 --> 00:09:54.150
is the same as what we're using in

